WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.884
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:05.190 --> 00:00:07.080
FRANCES PERRY: So
good afternoon.

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:08.660
My name is Frances Perry.

00:00:08.660 --> 00:00:10.340
I'm a software
engineer at Google,

00:00:10.340 --> 00:00:12.110
and I'm also on the
Project Management

00:00:12.110 --> 00:00:14.060
committee for Apache Beam.

00:00:14.060 --> 00:00:16.470
Now today I'm going to give
you an introduction to Apache

00:00:16.470 --> 00:00:20.285
Beam, which is a new open source
project focused on both batch

00:00:20.285 --> 00:00:22.460
and streaming use cases.

00:00:22.460 --> 00:00:24.230
Now when you're
using Beam you're

00:00:24.230 --> 00:00:26.960
really focusing on your
logic and your data

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:30.260
without letting details from
the runtime abstractions leak

00:00:30.260 --> 00:00:31.950
through into your code.

00:00:31.950 --> 00:00:35.510
And what that does is give
you a very clean separation.

00:00:35.510 --> 00:00:38.240
That means that your Beam
pipeline, once you've

00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:41.030
written it once, can be
run in multiple execution

00:00:41.030 --> 00:00:42.044
environments.

00:00:42.044 --> 00:00:43.460
Everything that
you know and love,

00:00:43.460 --> 00:00:46.250
whether it's Apache Spark,
Apache Flink, or Google Cloud

00:00:46.250 --> 00:00:47.590
Dataflow.

00:00:47.590 --> 00:00:50.240
Now to put Beam in context
and the surrounding big data

00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:53.760
ecosystem let's take a
quick look at the evolution.

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:56.120
So what you see here is that
original MapReduced paper

00:00:56.120 --> 00:00:57.640
in 2004.

00:00:57.640 --> 00:00:59.600
And this paper
fundamentally changed

00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:02.150
the way we do distributed
data processing.

00:01:02.150 --> 00:01:05.000
Now inside Google we
took that technology

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:06.770
and we continued to innovate.

00:01:06.770 --> 00:01:09.620
Publishing papers, building
systems, but really not

00:01:09.620 --> 00:01:11.810
sharing the code broadly.

00:01:11.810 --> 00:01:14.780
In 2014, we created
Google Cloud Dataflow,

00:01:14.780 --> 00:01:16.880
which is a part of
Google Cloud Platform

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:19.410
and based on all this
history internally.

00:01:19.410 --> 00:01:22.280
And this is basically both
a programming model in SDK

00:01:22.280 --> 00:01:25.220
for writing data parallel
processing pipelines,

00:01:25.220 --> 00:01:28.610
and also a fully managed
service for executing them.

00:01:28.610 --> 00:01:31.550
Now meanwhile, the
open source ecosystem

00:01:31.550 --> 00:01:34.230
took that MapReduced
paper and created Hadoop.

00:01:34.230 --> 00:01:38.450
And an entire ecosystem
flourished around this.

00:01:38.450 --> 00:01:41.240
Beam is essentially bringing
these two streams of work

00:01:41.240 --> 00:01:42.630
back together.

00:01:42.630 --> 00:01:44.870
It's based on the
programming model,

00:01:44.870 --> 00:01:47.630
but generalized and integrated
with the broader big data

00:01:47.630 --> 00:01:50.100
ecosystem.

00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:51.920
So today I'm going to
go into more detail

00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:53.720
on two key features of Beam.

00:01:53.720 --> 00:01:55.700
The first is the
programming model.

00:01:55.700 --> 00:01:58.100
These are the abstractions
at the heart of Beam

00:01:58.100 --> 00:02:00.200
that lets you express
these data parallel

00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:03.860
operations in a way that work
over both batch and streaming

00:02:03.860 --> 00:02:05.121
data.

00:02:05.121 --> 00:02:06.620
In addition, I'm
going to talk about

00:02:06.620 --> 00:02:10.610
the portability infrastructure
in the Apache Beam project.

00:02:10.610 --> 00:02:13.340
Now this is what lets you
execute the same Beam pipeline

00:02:13.340 --> 00:02:16.530
across multiple run times.

00:02:16.530 --> 00:02:18.870
After that we're going to
get a little more concrete,

00:02:18.870 --> 00:02:21.679
and I'll show you how these
concepts work in practice.

00:02:21.679 --> 00:02:23.220
There will be a demo
of the same Beam

00:02:23.220 --> 00:02:27.400
pipeline running across Apache
Spark, Apache Flank, and Cloud

00:02:27.400 --> 00:02:28.200
Dataflow.

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:30.660
And then I'll talk a
little bit about Talend

00:02:30.660 --> 00:02:33.600
and how they're using this
flexibility that Beam provides

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:34.889
in production.

00:02:34.889 --> 00:02:36.930
And finally at the end,
some pointers for getting

00:02:36.930 --> 00:02:39.490
started with using Apache Beam.

00:02:39.490 --> 00:02:42.510
All right so we'll start with
that overview of the Beam

00:02:42.510 --> 00:02:43.335
model.

00:02:43.335 --> 00:02:45.210
Now if you've been to
some of the other talks

00:02:45.210 --> 00:02:46.959
over the last few days,
you may have heard

00:02:46.959 --> 00:02:48.220
this example use a little bit.

00:02:48.220 --> 00:02:51.600
I think having a clear
example for explaining

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:53.390
difficult concepts
is really important.

00:02:53.390 --> 00:02:54.810
So we love this example.

00:02:54.810 --> 00:02:56.776
You'll hear about mobile
gaming quite a bit

00:02:56.776 --> 00:02:58.150
when you're in
the Dataflow talk.

00:02:58.150 --> 00:03:00.222
So I'll keep this version brief.

00:03:00.222 --> 00:03:02.180
If you want to learn
about this in more detail,

00:03:02.180 --> 00:03:04.920
you can go to some of the other
talks and watch the videos.

00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:06.420
So what we're going
to be doing here

00:03:06.420 --> 00:03:08.810
is pretending that we've
just launched a mobile game.

00:03:08.810 --> 00:03:11.100
So we've got users
all around the globe,

00:03:11.100 --> 00:03:13.890
on their mobile phones,
doing some sort of task

00:03:13.890 --> 00:03:15.720
to earn points for their team.

00:03:15.720 --> 00:03:17.640
Every time they
score a point this

00:03:17.640 --> 00:03:19.650
is sending this information
back to our server

00:03:19.650 --> 00:03:21.812
logs and we're
aggregating that data.

00:03:21.812 --> 00:03:23.520
We want to look through
those server logs

00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:27.160
and learn some patterns
about our users.

00:03:27.160 --> 00:03:29.310
So here we have a graph
of some sample data

00:03:29.310 --> 00:03:31.200
that we're going
to be working with.

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:33.330
On the x-axis you
have event time.

00:03:33.330 --> 00:03:36.690
This is basically the time
a user scored some points.

00:03:36.690 --> 00:03:39.850
And on the y-axis you
have processing time.

00:03:39.850 --> 00:03:41.280
This is the time that score--

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:44.830
that event-- arrived in
our system for processing.

00:03:44.830 --> 00:03:47.490
Now, what you'd expect is
that everything appears

00:03:47.490 --> 00:03:49.050
along this diagonal line.

00:03:49.050 --> 00:03:53.065
If distributed systems weren't
so gosh darn distributed things

00:03:53.065 --> 00:03:54.690
would arrive in our
system immediately.

00:03:54.690 --> 00:03:56.820
We'd be able to process
them and move on.

00:03:56.820 --> 00:03:59.700
But in reality of course
things don't work that way.

00:03:59.700 --> 00:04:02.970
So let's take a look at
the score of three here.

00:04:02.970 --> 00:04:04.260
This is pretty decent.

00:04:04.260 --> 00:04:06.030
This score is only
slightly delayed.

00:04:06.030 --> 00:04:09.660
It happens just before
12:07, arrives in our system

00:04:09.660 --> 00:04:12.100
just after 12:07 for processing.

00:04:12.100 --> 00:04:14.910
So this user's information was
able to get into the system

00:04:14.910 --> 00:04:16.660
pretty quickly.

00:04:16.660 --> 00:04:19.079
However, over here,
the score of nine

00:04:19.079 --> 00:04:20.640
looks about seven
minutes delayed.

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:23.280
It happened just after
12:01, but doesn't

00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:26.580
arrive in our system for
processing until about 12:08.

00:04:26.580 --> 00:04:30.270
So perhaps this user was playing
our game on the elevator,

00:04:30.270 --> 00:04:33.000
in the subway, somewhere where
they had a temporary lack

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:34.196
of network connectivity.

00:04:34.196 --> 00:04:35.820
So it took a little
bit for their phone

00:04:35.820 --> 00:04:38.280
to reconnect to the network,
and this information

00:04:38.280 --> 00:04:40.710
about the points that they'd
scored to be able to make it

00:04:40.710 --> 00:04:43.344
back to our system.

00:04:43.344 --> 00:04:44.760
So this graph of
course isn't even

00:04:44.760 --> 00:04:47.610
big enough to handle what would
happen if we had a real offline

00:04:47.610 --> 00:04:48.780
mode for our game.

00:04:48.780 --> 00:04:50.730
Where we've got users
on the middle seat

00:04:50.730 --> 00:04:53.040
of some transatlantic
flight playing our game

00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:54.697
for hours over the ocean.

00:04:54.697 --> 00:04:56.280
There we'd have to
wait for that plane

00:04:56.280 --> 00:04:59.190
to land, that user to
come out of airplane mode,

00:04:59.190 --> 00:05:01.560
and reconnect to a network
before we get events,

00:05:01.560 --> 00:05:04.290
potentially hours
and hours delayed.

00:05:04.290 --> 00:05:06.960
So these types of infinite
and out of order datasets

00:05:06.960 --> 00:05:08.880
can be really tricky
to reason about

00:05:08.880 --> 00:05:11.860
unless you know the
questions to ask.

00:05:11.860 --> 00:05:13.950
So the Beam model
really revolves

00:05:13.950 --> 00:05:16.030
around four key questions.

00:05:16.030 --> 00:05:18.970
The first one is, what
results are calculated?

00:05:18.970 --> 00:05:20.244
This is your business logic.

00:05:20.244 --> 00:05:21.660
This is the
algorithms that you're

00:05:21.660 --> 00:05:24.810
performing over your data,
whether it's sums, joins,

00:05:24.810 --> 00:05:27.210
histograms, machine
learning models.

00:05:27.210 --> 00:05:29.740
Whatever you're actually doing.

00:05:29.740 --> 00:05:32.670
Where in event time
are results calculated?

00:05:32.670 --> 00:05:35.550
This talks about how the
timed event actually occurred

00:05:35.550 --> 00:05:37.200
affects the results.

00:05:37.200 --> 00:05:40.020
Are we aggregating things
over all event time?

00:05:40.020 --> 00:05:42.060
Or are we trying
to do it in slices

00:05:42.060 --> 00:05:45.660
like hourly windows, daily
windows, or even bursts of user

00:05:45.660 --> 00:05:48.220
activity sessions.

00:05:48.220 --> 00:05:51.250
When in processing time
are results materialized?

00:05:51.250 --> 00:05:53.020
This talks about how
the time the elements

00:05:53.020 --> 00:05:55.900
actually make into the
system affect results.

00:05:55.900 --> 00:05:58.510
So how do we know when we've
seen all the data for a given

00:05:58.510 --> 00:06:01.720
time period, and we can go
ahead and emit a result?

00:06:01.720 --> 00:06:03.310
What do we do about
data that comes

00:06:03.310 --> 00:06:05.040
in late when those flights--

00:06:05.040 --> 00:06:06.600
those transatlantic
flights land?

00:06:06.600 --> 00:06:10.600
And we get more data way
after we thought we were done.

00:06:10.600 --> 00:06:11.710
And a final question.

00:06:11.710 --> 00:06:13.540
How do refinements relate?

00:06:13.540 --> 00:06:15.970
Talks about what we
do when we choose

00:06:15.970 --> 00:06:19.750
to emit multiple results as we
get higher and higher fidelity.

00:06:19.750 --> 00:06:22.690
Do those different versions of
the result build on each other

00:06:22.690 --> 00:06:25.239
or are they distinct.

00:06:25.239 --> 00:06:26.780
So I'm going to take
these questions,

00:06:26.780 --> 00:06:29.238
and we're going to build up a
pretty simple data processing

00:06:29.238 --> 00:06:31.340
pipeline to use
them to calculate

00:06:31.340 --> 00:06:36.410
hourly scores for the teams
playing our mobile game.

00:06:36.410 --> 00:06:39.050
So here we have a code
snippet from a pipeline that's

00:06:39.050 --> 00:06:40.872
processing those
scoring results.

00:06:40.872 --> 00:06:42.830
So we've already gone
ahead and parsed the data

00:06:42.830 --> 00:06:45.470
into some sort of structured
key value form, where we've

00:06:45.470 --> 00:06:48.170
got the team name and the
number of points that were just

00:06:48.170 --> 00:06:50.190
scored for that team.

00:06:50.190 --> 00:06:53.240
So in yellow you can see the
what question being answered.

00:06:53.240 --> 00:06:55.520
This is what we're
actually computing.

00:06:55.520 --> 00:06:58.160
And here we're taking
those key value pairs

00:06:58.160 --> 00:07:01.160
and we're summing the
integers per key or per team

00:07:01.160 --> 00:07:04.790
to get the total
score for that team.

00:07:04.790 --> 00:07:07.150
So let's take this code
snippet and go ahead and run it

00:07:07.150 --> 00:07:09.250
in a standard batch algorithm.

00:07:09.250 --> 00:07:11.110
Now in this looping
animation, you

00:07:11.110 --> 00:07:14.530
can see the gray line is
representing processing time.

00:07:14.530 --> 00:07:17.760
As the pipeline's executing
and processing elements

00:07:17.760 --> 00:07:20.260
and countering them, they're
accumulating those elements

00:07:20.260 --> 00:07:23.800
into that intermediate state
that's trailing the line.

00:07:23.800 --> 00:07:26.410
And when processing
completes, the system

00:07:26.410 --> 00:07:28.010
emits the result in yellow.

00:07:28.010 --> 00:07:29.641
So this is our final
result. And you

00:07:29.641 --> 00:07:31.390
can see here that we're
aggregating things

00:07:31.390 --> 00:07:32.560
regardless of event time.

00:07:32.560 --> 00:07:34.810
We're just sort of taken
all our data in one chunk

00:07:34.810 --> 00:07:36.280
and processing it.

00:07:36.280 --> 00:07:39.470
This is basically pretty
standard batch processing.

00:07:39.470 --> 00:07:41.300
But as we dive into
the remaining questions

00:07:41.300 --> 00:07:43.680
we'll see this start to change.

00:07:43.680 --> 00:07:46.980
So we'll start by
playing with event time.

00:07:46.980 --> 00:07:49.590
Here we're specifying
a windowing function

00:07:49.590 --> 00:07:52.710
that says we'd like that
same algorithm calculated

00:07:52.710 --> 00:07:55.830
independently for different
slices of event time.

00:07:55.830 --> 00:07:58.900
So for example we could do
this every minute, every hour,

00:07:58.900 --> 00:08:00.360
every day.

00:08:00.360 --> 00:08:02.670
And in this case, we're doing
it in two-minute windows.

00:08:02.670 --> 00:08:05.580
And so what we're going to see
when we go and run this is now

00:08:05.580 --> 00:08:09.540
we're calculating four distinct
results for our sample data,

00:08:09.540 --> 00:08:14.010
based on the event time of when
the event actually occurred.

00:08:14.010 --> 00:08:15.840
Now at this point
in this diagram

00:08:15.840 --> 00:08:18.780
though we're still waiting
for the entire computation

00:08:18.780 --> 00:08:21.840
to complete before
we emit any results.

00:08:21.840 --> 00:08:23.790
So that works fine
for a bounded data

00:08:23.790 --> 00:08:26.430
set when you have all the data
that you're ever going to have,

00:08:26.430 --> 00:08:27.888
and you know that
you're processing

00:08:27.888 --> 00:08:29.160
will eventually complete.

00:08:29.160 --> 00:08:30.618
But it's not going
to work if we're

00:08:30.618 --> 00:08:33.539
trying to process an
infinite amount of data.

00:08:33.539 --> 00:08:35.039
In that case, what
we want to do is

00:08:35.039 --> 00:08:38.640
start reducing the latency
of individual results.

00:08:38.640 --> 00:08:40.590
And we do that by
asking for a result

00:08:40.590 --> 00:08:43.740
to be triggered based on the
system's best estimate of when

00:08:43.740 --> 00:08:45.900
it's pretty much
seen all the data.

00:08:45.900 --> 00:08:50.510
We call this estimate of
completion the watermark.

00:08:50.510 --> 00:08:53.390
So now the same graph we've
been using before showing

00:08:53.390 --> 00:08:55.370
the watermark in green.

00:08:55.370 --> 00:08:57.770
And it's basically
estimating when the system

00:08:57.770 --> 00:08:59.270
thinks it has all the data.

00:08:59.270 --> 00:09:01.940
And you can see that as soon
as that watermark passes

00:09:01.940 --> 00:09:03.650
the end of the
window, we go ahead

00:09:03.650 --> 00:09:06.780
and give you the
result for that window.

00:09:06.780 --> 00:09:09.320
But again, the watermark
in a distributed system

00:09:09.320 --> 00:09:12.020
is really often going
to be just a heuristic.

00:09:12.020 --> 00:09:15.140
So here you can see that
the watermark is too fast.

00:09:15.140 --> 00:09:17.780
We think we've seen all the
data for that first window.

00:09:17.780 --> 00:09:19.481
We go ahead and
emit the results.

00:09:19.481 --> 00:09:21.730
And then that late score
that happened in the elevator

00:09:21.730 --> 00:09:23.270
or the subway comes in.

00:09:23.270 --> 00:09:24.980
And that user doesn't
get to contribute

00:09:24.980 --> 00:09:27.110
that really awesome
score of nine points

00:09:27.110 --> 00:09:29.630
to their team's total.

00:09:29.630 --> 00:09:31.284
So we don't want to
be too slow either.

00:09:31.284 --> 00:09:33.200
On the other hand, if
we were too conservative

00:09:33.200 --> 00:09:35.150
with the watermark
we were finding

00:09:35.150 --> 00:09:37.880
that we'd be introducing
unnecessary latency.

00:09:37.880 --> 00:09:40.760
It's really hard to tell
when that last plane is going

00:09:40.760 --> 00:09:43.220
to land, and that last user
who is playing at one o'clock

00:09:43.220 --> 00:09:45.178
this morning is going to
turn off airplane mode

00:09:45.178 --> 00:09:46.546
and send us their data.

00:09:46.546 --> 00:09:48.420
So if we're trying to
be overly conservative,

00:09:48.420 --> 00:09:51.720
we'll find we introduce
unnecessary latency.

00:09:51.720 --> 00:09:54.650
So what we can do about that
is use a more sophisticated

00:09:54.650 --> 00:09:55.400
trigger.

00:09:55.400 --> 00:09:57.020
And so in this
case, we're asking

00:09:57.020 --> 00:09:59.360
for both speculative
early firings

00:09:59.360 --> 00:10:01.400
as data is still trickling in.

00:10:01.400 --> 00:10:06.290
And also, updates to our
result if late elements arrive.

00:10:06.290 --> 00:10:09.260
So once we do this and we
asked for multiple results

00:10:09.260 --> 00:10:11.930
for a given window, now we
have to answer the question

00:10:11.930 --> 00:10:14.630
about how those results
relate to each other.

00:10:14.630 --> 00:10:16.520
And here we've chosen
just to accumulate.

00:10:16.520 --> 00:10:19.580
So basically each time we emit
a new result for this window,

00:10:19.580 --> 00:10:23.250
we'll get an even
more precise answer.

00:10:23.250 --> 00:10:24.880
So if you look
here now you'll see

00:10:24.880 --> 00:10:29.010
we get those multiple results
for each of the windows.

00:10:29.010 --> 00:10:31.040
Some windows, like
that second one,

00:10:31.040 --> 00:10:34.470
are introducing those
early speculative firings

00:10:34.470 --> 00:10:37.560
with the incomplete results just
as the data arrives to give us

00:10:37.560 --> 00:10:38.975
an idea of what's going on.

00:10:38.975 --> 00:10:40.350
This would be
particularly useful

00:10:40.350 --> 00:10:42.287
if you are windowing
daily for example,

00:10:42.287 --> 00:10:44.370
and you wanted to check
in halfway through the day

00:10:44.370 --> 00:10:46.790
and see how things are looking.

00:10:46.790 --> 00:10:49.460
There's one on time result
for each window when

00:10:49.460 --> 00:10:51.260
the watermark passes
the end of that window

00:10:51.260 --> 00:10:53.590
and we think OK, this
should be about it.

00:10:53.590 --> 00:10:57.380
And in that first window we're
also getting a late firing

00:10:57.380 --> 00:10:59.300
when we encounter that
late element of nine

00:10:59.300 --> 00:11:04.850
to update that we have
a more refined result.

00:11:04.850 --> 00:11:08.030
So what I want you to take
away from this section

00:11:08.030 --> 00:11:11.360
is that we had an algorithm,
and we had a lot of flexibility

00:11:11.360 --> 00:11:13.070
with how we ran an algorithm.

00:11:13.070 --> 00:11:15.440
So here it was a very,
very simple algorithm.

00:11:15.440 --> 00:11:18.200
It was just integers
summation, but the same thing

00:11:18.200 --> 00:11:21.380
holds for much more complex
chunks of user logic.

00:11:21.380 --> 00:11:23.120
You take that user
logic and then

00:11:23.120 --> 00:11:25.370
you tweak just a couple
of other lines around it

00:11:25.370 --> 00:11:28.050
to answer those other
three questions.

00:11:28.050 --> 00:11:30.440
And what that gives
you is the ability

00:11:30.440 --> 00:11:34.070
to cover the spectrum between
simple traditional batch

00:11:34.070 --> 00:11:35.600
and some pretty
complex streaming

00:11:35.600 --> 00:11:39.380
use cases all in a
single framework.

00:11:39.380 --> 00:11:42.260
So just like MapReduce and
the abstractions of map

00:11:42.260 --> 00:11:44.990
and reduce that they
implemented completely changed

00:11:44.990 --> 00:11:47.870
the way that we as a community
think about doing distributed

00:11:47.870 --> 00:11:50.060
parallel data
processing, we hope

00:11:50.060 --> 00:11:52.670
that the Beam model is going
to change the way that people

00:11:52.670 --> 00:11:57.280
think about batch and streaming
going forward into the future.

00:11:57.280 --> 00:11:59.530
All right so with that
brief introduction

00:11:59.530 --> 00:12:01.690
to the Beam model
behind us, let's talk

00:12:01.690 --> 00:12:04.000
about how this model
can be portable

00:12:04.000 --> 00:12:06.820
across multiple runtimes.

00:12:06.820 --> 00:12:09.191
So the heart of Beam
really is this model.

00:12:09.191 --> 00:12:11.440
These abstractions that we've
introduced for reasoning

00:12:11.440 --> 00:12:14.020
about the style of programming.

00:12:14.020 --> 00:12:16.540
Now on top of this model,
the Apache Beam project

00:12:16.540 --> 00:12:19.960
aims to have multiple
language specific SDKs.

00:12:19.960 --> 00:12:22.750
Developers have
very strong opinions

00:12:22.750 --> 00:12:24.730
about their language of choice.

00:12:24.730 --> 00:12:26.474
That's not a battle
I want to fight.

00:12:26.474 --> 00:12:28.640
We just want to be able to
give you the options that

00:12:28.640 --> 00:12:31.060
meet your needs.

00:12:31.060 --> 00:12:34.010
Next, we have multiple runners
for executing that Beam

00:12:34.010 --> 00:12:37.710
pipeline in the environment
that fits your needs.

00:12:37.710 --> 00:12:39.640
This might be on premise.

00:12:39.640 --> 00:12:41.020
It might be in the cloud.

00:12:41.020 --> 00:12:42.700
Might be open source.

00:12:42.700 --> 00:12:44.410
Might be fully managed.

00:12:44.410 --> 00:12:46.245
Whatever your current needs are.

00:12:46.245 --> 00:12:47.620
And in fact those
needs are going

00:12:47.620 --> 00:12:51.550
to change over time as your
data evolves, your company

00:12:51.550 --> 00:12:54.599
evolves, the technologies
that you want to use evolve.

00:12:54.599 --> 00:12:56.140
So if we want to
give you the freedom

00:12:56.140 --> 00:12:59.050
to change that and take that
same logic that you've invested

00:12:59.050 --> 00:13:01.630
so much time in building,
and be able to run it

00:13:01.630 --> 00:13:04.250
in those different environments.

00:13:04.250 --> 00:13:06.874
Now, this diagram gets a
little more complicated,

00:13:06.874 --> 00:13:08.290
because what each
of those runners

00:13:08.290 --> 00:13:11.680
is doing when it executes
your pipeline is calling back

00:13:11.680 --> 00:13:13.890
into those language
specific SDKs

00:13:13.890 --> 00:13:17.140
to execute your per element
processing-- your map

00:13:17.140 --> 00:13:18.970
functions basically.

00:13:18.970 --> 00:13:21.550
So what we've done is
a project is built out

00:13:21.550 --> 00:13:25.300
a second layer of internal APIs
that really cleanly separate

00:13:25.300 --> 00:13:28.570
the runners and how they're
organizing and distributing

00:13:28.570 --> 00:13:32.230
the processing from the per
element processing that's

00:13:32.230 --> 00:13:34.060
written in those
custom languages.

00:13:34.060 --> 00:13:36.970
And by doing that we make it
very easy for the community

00:13:36.970 --> 00:13:38.960
to evolve these
components in parallel.

00:13:38.960 --> 00:13:41.530
So you can add a new runner
without knowing details

00:13:41.530 --> 00:13:43.180
about each of
those SDKs, and you

00:13:43.180 --> 00:13:45.880
can add a new SDK without
knowing the guts of how

00:13:45.880 --> 00:13:47.430
those runners work.

00:13:47.430 --> 00:13:50.410
And getting this layer correct
is incredibly important

00:13:50.410 --> 00:13:52.720
because this is the
data intensive path.

00:13:52.720 --> 00:13:54.730
This is the path that's
happening essentially

00:13:54.730 --> 00:13:57.635
per element or per bundle
at a very fine grain.

00:13:57.635 --> 00:14:00.010
And so you need to be able to
get this in the right place

00:14:00.010 --> 00:14:02.134
and be able to get the
performance that people need

00:14:02.134 --> 00:14:04.800
out of these big data systems.

00:14:04.800 --> 00:14:06.620
All right so this
is the Beam vision.

00:14:06.620 --> 00:14:09.460
This is what Apache
Beam is aiming to be.

00:14:09.460 --> 00:14:11.050
We're not there yet.

00:14:11.050 --> 00:14:12.400
This is where we are.

00:14:12.400 --> 00:14:15.794
So currently we
have the Java SDK.

00:14:15.794 --> 00:14:17.710
It's quite mature, and
it runs across a number

00:14:17.710 --> 00:14:18.970
of different runners.

00:14:18.970 --> 00:14:21.430
So Spark, Flink,
Dataflow and Apex

00:14:21.430 --> 00:14:24.010
are on the main master branch,
and we have Apache Gear Pump

00:14:24.010 --> 00:14:25.660
under development.

00:14:25.660 --> 00:14:30.280
Now the Python SDK has also just
merged into the master branch.

00:14:30.280 --> 00:14:33.790
But we don't yet have that
bottom level of the function

00:14:33.790 --> 00:14:37.192
portability layer in place to
allow the Python SDK to run

00:14:37.192 --> 00:14:38.400
across all the other runners.

00:14:38.400 --> 00:14:41.830
So currently we only have
support for the Python SDK

00:14:41.830 --> 00:14:44.500
to run its scale in batch
mode on Cloud Dataflow.

00:14:44.500 --> 00:14:47.950
So we're actively working
towards building that out.

00:14:50.619 --> 00:14:52.160
So let's go into a
little more detail

00:14:52.160 --> 00:14:53.440
about some of these runners.

00:14:53.440 --> 00:14:55.730
So these three were
the original runners

00:14:55.730 --> 00:14:57.500
that were part of the
Apache Beam project.

00:14:57.500 --> 00:14:59.000
And they're also
the three I'm going

00:14:59.000 --> 00:15:01.340
to be demoing in just a minute.

00:15:01.340 --> 00:15:03.860
So many of you are probably
familiar with Apache Spark.

00:15:03.860 --> 00:15:06.800
It's a very popular choice
right now in the big data world.

00:15:06.800 --> 00:15:09.350
And it really excels in
memory and interactive style

00:15:09.350 --> 00:15:11.350
computations.

00:15:11.350 --> 00:15:13.980
Apache Flink is a bit more
of a newcomer to the broader

00:15:13.980 --> 00:15:15.510
big data ecosystem.

00:15:15.510 --> 00:15:18.040
But it has some really clean
and excellent semantics

00:15:18.040 --> 00:15:20.550
for doing stream processing.

00:15:20.550 --> 00:15:22.200
And, finally, Cloud
Dataflow, this

00:15:22.200 --> 00:15:24.480
is Google Cloud platform's
fully managed service

00:15:24.480 --> 00:15:25.650
for data processing.

00:15:25.650 --> 00:15:28.110
And this evolved along
with sort of the technology

00:15:28.110 --> 00:15:31.050
at the heart of Beam in
those years of internal work

00:15:31.050 --> 00:15:33.900
at Google.

00:15:33.900 --> 00:15:38.430
So each of these runners does
parallel data processing.

00:15:38.430 --> 00:15:41.400
But they each do them in
a slightly different way.

00:15:41.400 --> 00:15:43.200
So the question is,
how are we going

00:15:43.200 --> 00:15:45.120
to build an
abstraction layer that

00:15:45.120 --> 00:15:47.190
takes these very
different things

00:15:47.190 --> 00:15:49.620
and generalizes
them into one thing?

00:15:49.620 --> 00:15:52.200
So one option would
be to just take

00:15:52.200 --> 00:15:55.050
the intersection of what all
the different runners can do.

00:15:55.050 --> 00:15:57.376
But that's not going to be
nearly interesting enough.

00:15:57.376 --> 00:15:59.250
That's just going to be
a small slice of what

00:15:59.250 --> 00:16:02.632
data processing is capable of.

00:16:02.632 --> 00:16:04.590
On the other hand if we
tried to take the union

00:16:04.590 --> 00:16:06.390
and include all the
functionality that all

00:16:06.390 --> 00:16:10.050
the runners possibly have, we'd
end up with a real big kitchen

00:16:10.050 --> 00:16:11.350
sink of chaos.

00:16:11.350 --> 00:16:13.620
It'd be really complicated,
hard to understand.

00:16:13.620 --> 00:16:17.100
It wouldn't be this clean
crisp programming model that

00:16:17.100 --> 00:16:19.320
makes these complex tasks easy.

00:16:19.320 --> 00:16:21.540
And that's really
what we're after.

00:16:21.540 --> 00:16:23.550
So instead what we're
trying to do with

00:16:23.550 --> 00:16:27.840
Beam is be at the forefront of
where data processing is going.

00:16:27.840 --> 00:16:30.900
Both pushing functionality
into and pulling

00:16:30.900 --> 00:16:34.680
patterns out of those
underlying runtime engines.

00:16:34.680 --> 00:16:36.990
So keyed state is
a great example

00:16:36.990 --> 00:16:39.480
of a feature that existed
in some of these engines

00:16:39.480 --> 00:16:40.320
for a while.

00:16:40.320 --> 00:16:43.290
It was very useful for
stream processing use cases.

00:16:43.290 --> 00:16:45.480
But it didn't exist
in Beam originally.

00:16:45.480 --> 00:16:47.430
So we've worked with
the Beam community

00:16:47.430 --> 00:16:49.290
to understand how keyed
state is implemented

00:16:49.290 --> 00:16:50.820
across these different runners.

00:16:50.820 --> 00:16:52.440
And really pull
up an abstraction

00:16:52.440 --> 00:16:54.240
and a notion of
this feature that

00:16:54.240 --> 00:16:56.820
fits cleanly in the Beam
model and integrates nicely

00:16:56.820 --> 00:16:59.970
with our other semantics.

00:16:59.970 --> 00:17:01.910
On the other hand, we
also hope that Beam

00:17:01.910 --> 00:17:03.630
will influence the
roadmaps of each

00:17:03.630 --> 00:17:05.520
of these individual engines.

00:17:05.520 --> 00:17:07.530
So for example, the
semantics that Flink

00:17:07.530 --> 00:17:09.540
uses right now for
their data streams

00:17:09.540 --> 00:17:11.760
was heavily influenced by
a lot of the early work

00:17:11.760 --> 00:17:12.801
we did on the Beam model.

00:17:15.329 --> 00:17:17.119
This also means though
at times that there

00:17:17.119 --> 00:17:20.030
may be a divergence between
the general power of the Beam

00:17:20.030 --> 00:17:22.430
model, and what different
runners are currently

00:17:22.430 --> 00:17:24.104
capable of implementing.

00:17:24.104 --> 00:17:26.270
Because we're going to pull
something into the model

00:17:26.270 --> 00:17:27.980
when we are convinced
it's the right way

00:17:27.980 --> 00:17:29.960
for data processing
to move forward.

00:17:29.960 --> 00:17:33.395
Not all of the runners may
be able to build on that yet.

00:17:33.395 --> 00:17:35.270
So what we're going to
do in the Beam project

00:17:35.270 --> 00:17:38.000
is track that very clearly on
the website and the capability

00:17:38.000 --> 00:17:39.320
matrix.

00:17:39.320 --> 00:17:42.681
And I don't think you should
take this as an example

00:17:42.681 --> 00:17:44.930
that some of these runners
just aren't worth anything.

00:17:44.930 --> 00:17:46.610
I think what it
means is that there

00:17:46.610 --> 00:17:48.950
are significant use
cases that don't

00:17:48.950 --> 00:17:50.720
need some of these features.

00:17:50.720 --> 00:17:53.510
Batch processing is something
we've all been doing for years.

00:17:53.510 --> 00:17:55.660
All of these runners
are great at that.

00:17:55.660 --> 00:17:58.430
Some of the more
semantic differences

00:17:58.430 --> 00:18:00.230
between event time
and processing time

00:18:00.230 --> 00:18:03.020
are still really filtering
out into the broader big data

00:18:03.020 --> 00:18:04.250
ecosystem as a whole.

00:18:04.250 --> 00:18:06.860
And you're seeing these engines
make significant improvements

00:18:06.860 --> 00:18:08.870
towards those things over time.

00:18:08.870 --> 00:18:10.790
So our goal as a
project is really

00:18:10.790 --> 00:18:13.460
to be establishing the
high watermark of what

00:18:13.460 --> 00:18:16.070
data processing should be,
and helping everyone else

00:18:16.070 --> 00:18:17.020
implement that well.

00:18:20.440 --> 00:18:22.540
All right, so with those
concepts behind us.

00:18:22.540 --> 00:18:24.610
A brief overview of the
model and the discussion

00:18:24.610 --> 00:18:25.750
of portability.

00:18:25.750 --> 00:18:28.760
I'd like to get hands
on and dive into a demo.

00:18:28.760 --> 00:18:30.850
So what we're going to do
now is swap to my laptop

00:18:30.850 --> 00:18:34.630
and actually see some
Beam pipeline in progress.

00:18:34.630 --> 00:18:39.370
So what we have here is a very
simple batch pipeline that's

00:18:39.370 --> 00:18:42.160
taking a bunch of those server
logs where each line represents

00:18:42.160 --> 00:18:44.330
a user scoring points
for their team,

00:18:44.330 --> 00:18:47.560
and we're trying to calculate
the hourly team scores.

00:18:47.560 --> 00:18:49.960
So per hour, windowed
by hour, what

00:18:49.960 --> 00:18:54.082
did each team score across
all the users on that team.

00:18:54.082 --> 00:18:55.540
So you can see here
we're starting.

00:18:55.540 --> 00:18:57.119
We're going to parse
some arguments.

00:18:57.119 --> 00:18:58.660
Then tell the system
that we're going

00:18:58.660 --> 00:19:00.390
to start creating a pipeline.

00:19:00.390 --> 00:19:01.736
Right here.

00:19:01.736 --> 00:19:04.360
And then once we do that, we're
building the pipeline structure

00:19:04.360 --> 00:19:07.070
by adding transformations to it.

00:19:07.070 --> 00:19:11.050
So we'll start by reading
new line delimited text

00:19:11.050 --> 00:19:12.465
from an input file.

00:19:12.465 --> 00:19:14.590
And then we'll take each
of those lines of new line

00:19:14.590 --> 00:19:17.120
delimited text and
go off and parse it.

00:19:17.120 --> 00:19:19.220
So this is basically
the map functions

00:19:19.220 --> 00:19:21.170
that you know and love.

00:19:21.170 --> 00:19:22.660
I happen to be
using Java 7 here.

00:19:22.660 --> 00:19:24.790
If I was using Java 8
there's an lambdas and things

00:19:24.790 --> 00:19:27.470
we can be doing as well.

00:19:27.470 --> 00:19:30.460
But if you dive into the body of
this user processing function,

00:19:30.460 --> 00:19:32.590
you'll see that it's an
element wise processing.

00:19:32.590 --> 00:19:34.240
It's taking a string
in this case, that

00:19:34.240 --> 00:19:36.670
would be that line of
long text, and turning it

00:19:36.670 --> 00:19:38.530
into a more structured format--

00:19:38.530 --> 00:19:40.330
a game action info object--

00:19:40.330 --> 00:19:43.390
so that we're able to more
easily interact with it.

00:19:43.390 --> 00:19:46.122
So for each element we're
going to grab the element,

00:19:46.122 --> 00:19:47.580
we're going to
split it [INAUDIBLE]

00:19:47.580 --> 00:19:49.754
and try to extract
those pieces, and you'll

00:19:49.754 --> 00:19:51.670
see that at the end there
we're going to build

00:19:51.670 --> 00:19:53.530
that more structured format.

00:19:53.530 --> 00:19:57.280
Now as we're going, we might
encounter a parse error.

00:19:57.280 --> 00:19:59.500
In fact will almost certainly
encounter a parse error

00:19:59.500 --> 00:20:02.690
since I sneakily injected
some into the input data.

00:20:02.690 --> 00:20:04.570
And so what we want to
do there is go ahead

00:20:04.570 --> 00:20:05.880
and catch that exception.

00:20:05.880 --> 00:20:08.925
And we're going to increment
a counter, log the method,

00:20:08.925 --> 00:20:09.550
and keep going.

00:20:09.550 --> 00:20:11.200
Because we're going to
want to build a pipeline

00:20:11.200 --> 00:20:13.150
that is resilient to
these kinds of things,

00:20:13.150 --> 00:20:14.941
because you can never
trust your input data

00:20:14.941 --> 00:20:16.940
to be as clean as you'd like.

00:20:16.940 --> 00:20:19.360
So with that parse
structure, let's go back down

00:20:19.360 --> 00:20:21.020
to the main method.

00:20:21.020 --> 00:20:23.740
So now you can see we're
going to set the time stamps.

00:20:23.740 --> 00:20:25.900
And what this will do
is take that time stamp

00:20:25.900 --> 00:20:28.480
that we parsed out
of the log line

00:20:28.480 --> 00:20:31.870
and set it as the metadata
time stamp on that element.

00:20:31.870 --> 00:20:34.630
And this allows the system
to interact with the time

00:20:34.630 --> 00:20:35.680
stamp of that element.

00:20:35.680 --> 00:20:38.080
And it will use that
information to do things

00:20:38.080 --> 00:20:42.430
like adjust the way windowing
doing and triggering behave.

00:20:42.430 --> 00:20:45.790
Next, we'll go ahead and ask
to calculate one hour fixed

00:20:45.790 --> 00:20:48.460
windows of our answer.

00:20:48.460 --> 00:20:51.340
And then we'll ask to
run our calculate team

00:20:51.340 --> 00:20:52.720
scores transform.

00:20:52.720 --> 00:20:55.150
Now this is a composite
transform that we wrote.

00:20:55.150 --> 00:20:58.750
That basically means it's a
sub graph of more information.

00:20:58.750 --> 00:21:01.810
And this sub graph is going to
take a [INAUDIBLE] collection

00:21:01.810 --> 00:21:05.170
of those game action infos and
return as an MDP collection.

00:21:05.170 --> 00:21:06.670
Because it's
actually-- a collection

00:21:06.670 --> 00:21:08.586
void-- because it's
actually going to go ahead

00:21:08.586 --> 00:21:10.270
and write them
straight out to files.

00:21:10.270 --> 00:21:12.580
And the body here is that
we're going to extract

00:21:12.580 --> 00:21:14.630
the team and the score.

00:21:14.630 --> 00:21:17.470
And then we're going to sum
those integers per team,

00:21:17.470 --> 00:21:20.649
so that we have the score
for that given team.

00:21:20.649 --> 00:21:22.690
And then we're going to
go ahead and write it out

00:21:22.690 --> 00:21:27.240
to a file with
one file per hour.

00:21:27.240 --> 00:21:30.540
So that's basically the
structure of this pipeline.

00:21:30.540 --> 00:21:32.900
So let's pop over for a
minute and take a look

00:21:32.900 --> 00:21:34.000
at the input data.

00:21:34.000 --> 00:21:37.200
So I'm going to run over
a handful of CSV files.

00:21:37.200 --> 00:21:39.660
Each of them 12 to 30 gigs.

00:21:39.660 --> 00:21:43.730
So a decent amount of
data, not a huge amount.

00:21:43.730 --> 00:21:46.550
And then when we go and
execute that pipeline,

00:21:46.550 --> 00:21:50.180
here I am running this
pipeline on Cloud Dataflow.

00:21:50.180 --> 00:21:53.510
So what you can see here is this
is the structure of that code

00:21:53.510 --> 00:21:56.870
I built. So there's our read,
our parse, our set time stamps.

00:21:56.870 --> 00:22:00.145
Here's our composite operation
down here the sum team scores.

00:22:00.145 --> 00:22:01.520
And you can see
it gives us a way

00:22:01.520 --> 00:22:04.670
to pop in and see that internal
structure that we built.

00:22:04.670 --> 00:22:07.880
This way of sort of expressing
that composite structure

00:22:07.880 --> 00:22:10.917
of your pipeline to your
monitoring UI is hugely useful.

00:22:10.917 --> 00:22:13.250
If you've ever seen systems
that are letting you monitor

00:22:13.250 --> 00:22:14.870
these pipelines, the DAG
that you're looking at

00:22:14.870 --> 00:22:17.030
is like this big and it's
completely impossible

00:22:17.030 --> 00:22:18.570
to make any sense of it.

00:22:18.570 --> 00:22:21.530
So this basically lets
you as a programmer--

00:22:21.530 --> 00:22:23.390
you're used to thinking
modularly-- this

00:22:23.390 --> 00:22:26.240
lets you add that structure
into your pipeline in a way

00:22:26.240 --> 00:22:30.360
that the system is able
to speak it back to you.

00:22:30.360 --> 00:22:33.120
So you can see that this job
is running with the Apache Beam

00:22:33.120 --> 00:22:34.147
SDK for Java 06.

00:22:34.147 --> 00:22:35.730
This is actually the
release candidate

00:22:35.730 --> 00:22:37.950
of next week's release--

00:22:37.950 --> 00:22:39.324
ran for nine minutes.

00:22:39.324 --> 00:22:40.740
You'll see that
some of the stages

00:22:40.740 --> 00:22:43.800
were reported that they
ran for longer than that.

00:22:43.800 --> 00:22:46.050
That's because this is summed
up over all the workers

00:22:46.050 --> 00:22:47.516
that were actually executing.

00:22:47.516 --> 00:22:48.890
So down here you
can see Dataflow

00:22:48.890 --> 00:22:54.511
chose auto scale to 15 workers
to run the size of data.

00:22:54.511 --> 00:22:56.760
If we scroll down, you can
also see those parse errors

00:22:56.760 --> 00:22:58.680
that we were counting
are tracked here.

00:22:58.680 --> 00:23:00.664
So we can see, as the
pipeline's executing--

00:23:00.664 --> 00:23:02.330
this is already
completed before running

00:23:02.330 --> 00:23:05.400
and see these ticking up
as we were encountering

00:23:05.400 --> 00:23:07.484
those parse errors.

00:23:07.484 --> 00:23:09.150
So that's this just
running on Dataflow.

00:23:09.150 --> 00:23:12.736
We can go we can check
our output directory

00:23:12.736 --> 00:23:13.860
for the Dataflow Directory.

00:23:13.860 --> 00:23:15.693
You can look and you
can see basically we're

00:23:15.693 --> 00:23:17.880
getting one input
file per hour here.

00:23:17.880 --> 00:23:21.780
We can pop in and see
there's the team names.

00:23:21.780 --> 00:23:23.469
There's the scores
for those teams.

00:23:23.469 --> 00:23:24.510
I made up the team names.

00:23:24.510 --> 00:23:26.774
So there you go.

00:23:26.774 --> 00:23:28.190
So that's what the
pipeline looked

00:23:28.190 --> 00:23:29.630
like running on Dataflow.

00:23:29.630 --> 00:23:31.900
But I also want to take
that exact same pipeline,

00:23:31.900 --> 00:23:34.400
and I'm going to run it
on two other runners.

00:23:34.400 --> 00:23:37.520
So over here I have
the Spark runner.

00:23:37.520 --> 00:23:39.470
So I've got a Spark
cluster up and I've

00:23:39.470 --> 00:23:42.290
run out that exact
same pipeline.

00:23:42.290 --> 00:23:46.130
You can see that as we
dive into here, basically

00:23:46.130 --> 00:23:49.340
this is Spark's definition
of that high level DAG.

00:23:49.340 --> 00:23:52.260
So what you're seeing here is
basically that original map

00:23:52.260 --> 00:23:54.110
and parsing phase.

00:23:54.110 --> 00:23:56.000
Then there's that
first combine that

00:23:56.000 --> 00:23:58.059
comes from the summing
across the keys.

00:23:58.059 --> 00:23:59.600
And this was that
second group by key

00:23:59.600 --> 00:24:02.970
that we're using to
organize the output files.

00:24:02.970 --> 00:24:05.780
So if we go down of
those three phases really

00:24:05.780 --> 00:24:08.240
the one with all the guts
in it is this first one.

00:24:08.240 --> 00:24:10.220
Because any distributed
processing engine worth

00:24:10.220 --> 00:24:12.320
its salt does as
much pre-combining

00:24:12.320 --> 00:24:14.354
as it can before it
starts shuffling.

00:24:14.354 --> 00:24:16.520
So when you go down and
look at the comparative size

00:24:16.520 --> 00:24:20.910
of these three stages all
the goodness is in here.

00:24:20.910 --> 00:24:23.210
So now you're starting
to see that the DAG

00:24:23.210 --> 00:24:24.830
for this stage that
looks more like

00:24:24.830 --> 00:24:26.150
the code that we were writing.

00:24:26.150 --> 00:24:29.810
So there's our read, we
got our parse game here,

00:24:29.810 --> 00:24:33.632
setting our time stamps, and
down to that pre-combine.

00:24:33.632 --> 00:24:35.090
And if you dig in
here again you'll

00:24:35.090 --> 00:24:37.090
see the shuffle of data
that's being written out

00:24:37.090 --> 00:24:38.015
is relatively small.

00:24:40.620 --> 00:24:43.100
If we look into the
event timeline a bit

00:24:43.100 --> 00:24:46.467
you can see each of the parallel
workers setting up their task--

00:24:46.467 --> 00:24:48.050
going ahead and
processing their data.

00:24:48.050 --> 00:24:51.750
It takes a little bit
variable amount of time.

00:24:51.750 --> 00:24:52.250
And so on.

00:24:52.250 --> 00:24:55.100
So one thing that's a little bit
difficult to see in the Spark

00:24:55.100 --> 00:24:57.127
UI is the amount of input data.

00:24:57.127 --> 00:24:59.210
So if you look, it looks
like none of these stages

00:24:59.210 --> 00:25:01.293
are actually reading any
data because this is only

00:25:01.293 --> 00:25:04.520
tracking data read to and
from Hadoop and Spark storage.

00:25:04.520 --> 00:25:07.322
In this case, I'm using
GCS to store my data.

00:25:07.322 --> 00:25:09.780
It doesn't matter which of
these runners you're running on.

00:25:09.780 --> 00:25:12.080
We have a unified I/O API.

00:25:12.080 --> 00:25:14.390
That means that you can
use that same I/O connector

00:25:14.390 --> 00:25:16.130
across all of the Beam runners.

00:25:16.130 --> 00:25:20.525
The Beam runners just implement
support for the generic Beam

00:25:20.525 --> 00:25:24.720
I/O, and then they're able to
get all of this functionality.

00:25:24.720 --> 00:25:26.810
If we swap over to
the Flink runner,

00:25:26.810 --> 00:25:29.840
so here's the same job again
running on Apache Flink

00:25:29.840 --> 00:25:32.630
I pop in like that
again there's a DAG.

00:25:32.630 --> 00:25:34.564
Starting to feel familiar.

00:25:34.564 --> 00:25:36.480
So you can see the DAG
a little bit different.

00:25:36.480 --> 00:25:41.250
Here we have the read in Flink
There's our parse events.

00:25:41.250 --> 00:25:44.180
Our combines coming
up over there.

00:25:44.180 --> 00:25:46.610
Here you can really see
the data sizes show up

00:25:46.610 --> 00:25:49.220
a little better as the
transitions between stages.

00:25:49.220 --> 00:25:53.420
So there's 109 gigs sent from
that first stage in Flink

00:25:53.420 --> 00:25:54.200
to the second one.

00:25:54.200 --> 00:25:56.960
You can see the number of
records that are involved

00:25:56.960 --> 00:25:59.394
here a little more heavily.

00:25:59.394 --> 00:26:01.310
And if you go up and
look in the accumulators,

00:26:01.310 --> 00:26:05.132
again you can see in
that parse events.

00:26:05.132 --> 00:26:07.340
There's the number of errors
that we're encountering.

00:26:07.340 --> 00:26:09.740
So you're really able to see
that this same pipeline is

00:26:09.740 --> 00:26:13.980
running at scale across all
of these different engines.

00:26:13.980 --> 00:26:15.500
So at this point,
what I've shown

00:26:15.500 --> 00:26:17.240
you is just that batch
pipeline running.

00:26:17.240 --> 00:26:21.180
I want to also show you
a streaming pipeline.

00:26:21.180 --> 00:26:24.530
So here I have a second
main method that's built up.

00:26:24.530 --> 00:26:26.090
This time it's a
streaming pipeline,

00:26:26.090 --> 00:26:28.847
and it's going to read
from Google Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:26:28.847 --> 00:26:30.680
So it's basically reading
an infinite amount

00:26:30.680 --> 00:26:33.890
of data that will just
continue coming and coming.

00:26:33.890 --> 00:26:36.410
We're going to use
that exact same parsing

00:26:36.410 --> 00:26:38.840
logic that we had before.

00:26:38.840 --> 00:26:41.510
This time we don't have to
set the time stamps by hand.

00:26:41.510 --> 00:26:44.922
The system is able to gather
them directly out of Pub/Sub.

00:26:44.922 --> 00:26:46.880
And we're going to use
a slightly more advanced

00:26:46.880 --> 00:26:49.130
windowing algorithm, because we
want to take this and transform

00:26:49.130 --> 00:26:50.180
it into streaming mode.

00:26:50.180 --> 00:26:53.115
We don't just want to wait
for everything to complete

00:26:53.115 --> 00:26:53.990
to get those results.

00:26:53.990 --> 00:26:55.650
We want that low latency.

00:26:55.650 --> 00:26:58.160
So here we're going to ask
to trigger the windows when

00:26:58.160 --> 00:27:00.260
the watermark is past
the end of the window.

00:27:00.260 --> 00:27:03.260
And also ask for some
early speculative firings.

00:27:03.260 --> 00:27:06.680
And that late firing every
time we see a late element.

00:27:06.680 --> 00:27:09.230
But then after that, after
that initial slightly different

00:27:09.230 --> 00:27:11.990
initial setup, we're going to
run that same calculate team

00:27:11.990 --> 00:27:14.670
scores transform that
we've written before.

00:27:14.670 --> 00:27:16.745
And again, this is a
decently simple one so

00:27:16.745 --> 00:27:18.036
that I could explain it to you.

00:27:18.036 --> 00:27:20.480
But it doesn't matter the kinds
of complex logic you have.

00:27:20.480 --> 00:27:23.720
You can reuse that same
logic across these two modes.

00:27:23.720 --> 00:27:25.540
Across both bounded
fixed size data

00:27:25.540 --> 00:27:28.580
sets and unbounded data sets.

00:27:28.580 --> 00:27:30.830
So if we take this
you can pop over

00:27:30.830 --> 00:27:33.810
and we're going to see it
running here in Dataflow.

00:27:33.810 --> 00:27:35.720
So here's that
same pipeline with

00:27:35.720 --> 00:27:39.960
that same composite
transform running down there.

00:27:39.960 --> 00:27:42.440
And you can see now
it's a streaming job.

00:27:42.440 --> 00:27:44.320
It's been running
since yesterday.

00:27:44.320 --> 00:27:46.070
It's only chosen to
use three workers.

00:27:46.070 --> 00:27:47.720
The auto scaling just
stopped at three here,

00:27:47.720 --> 00:27:50.303
because I'm injecting the data
with a single threaded machine.

00:27:50.303 --> 00:27:53.090
So there's not a
huge load of data.

00:27:53.090 --> 00:27:56.240
But it's processing nearly
2000 elements per second.

00:27:56.240 --> 00:27:59.711
You can see since yesterday
it's processed 43 gigs of data.

00:27:59.711 --> 00:28:02.210
And one of the things that's
most important in the streaming

00:28:02.210 --> 00:28:05.090
pipeline is to keep up
with the amount of data

00:28:05.090 --> 00:28:06.092
that's coming in.

00:28:06.092 --> 00:28:08.300
As you can see here we're
tracking the data watermark

00:28:08.300 --> 00:28:09.770
at 15:07.

00:28:09.770 --> 00:28:12.680
It's 15:08, so
that's pretty decent.

00:28:12.680 --> 00:28:17.290
We're keeping up with real
time as we're processing this.

00:28:17.290 --> 00:28:19.870
All right, so that's the
demo that I've got today.

00:28:19.870 --> 00:28:23.420
So let's go ahead and
pop back to the slides.

00:28:23.420 --> 00:28:26.480
So now I'd like to show you,
or talk about a little bit,

00:28:26.480 --> 00:28:30.800
is a customer actually able
to use this in production?

00:28:30.800 --> 00:28:33.560
So Talend is a
Google Cloud partner

00:28:33.560 --> 00:28:37.070
and they build open source big
data integration solutions.

00:28:37.070 --> 00:28:40.130
So Talend Data Preparation
is a self-service solution

00:28:40.130 --> 00:28:43.640
that enables their customers
to access, cleanse, and analyze

00:28:43.640 --> 00:28:44.850
their data.

00:28:44.850 --> 00:28:47.270
And it's available as both an
open source release and also

00:28:47.270 --> 00:28:49.230
an enterprise version.

00:28:49.230 --> 00:28:51.830
They also have a new product in
development called Talend Data

00:28:51.830 --> 00:28:52.920
Streams.

00:28:52.920 --> 00:28:55.500
And this is a UI that makes
it easy to build both batch

00:28:55.500 --> 00:28:57.620
and streaming data
processing pipelines.

00:28:57.620 --> 00:29:00.417
And it includes functionality
like interactive data analysis.

00:29:00.417 --> 00:29:02.000
So as you're
constructing the pipeline

00:29:02.000 --> 00:29:03.957
you can see samples,
sort of states

00:29:03.957 --> 00:29:06.290
of the intermediate collections
as you're building them.

00:29:06.290 --> 00:29:08.415
And it includes other things
like schema management

00:29:08.415 --> 00:29:09.920
and versioning.

00:29:09.920 --> 00:29:13.610
So Talend is building these
products on top of Apache Beam,

00:29:13.610 --> 00:29:15.380
because they believe
that building on Beam

00:29:15.380 --> 00:29:17.330
will allow them
and their customers

00:29:17.330 --> 00:29:19.940
to follow the evolution
of technologies.

00:29:19.940 --> 00:29:21.710
They'll be able to
leverage their data

00:29:21.710 --> 00:29:24.020
in the same way over
time while still

00:29:24.020 --> 00:29:27.530
having the flexibility to
execute on multiple runtimes

00:29:27.530 --> 00:29:28.590
as their needs change.

00:29:32.630 --> 00:29:35.170
So I'd like to talk a
little bit about getting

00:29:35.170 --> 00:29:37.420
started with Apache Beam.

00:29:37.420 --> 00:29:39.814
So since you're at
GCP Next I think

00:29:39.814 --> 00:29:41.230
there's a decent
chance that you'd

00:29:41.230 --> 00:29:44.530
be interested in running this
technology on Google Cloud.

00:29:44.530 --> 00:29:47.140
So what that means is that
Cloud Dataflow is likely going

00:29:47.140 --> 00:29:49.570
to be your choice of runner.

00:29:49.570 --> 00:29:52.325
Now we've been bootstrapping
Beam over the last year or so.

00:29:52.325 --> 00:29:54.450
So you'll find that when
you come to Cloud Dataflow

00:29:54.450 --> 00:29:57.460
you have a few different
options for running.

00:29:57.460 --> 00:30:00.190
We have the original
Dataflow SDK for Java,

00:30:00.190 --> 00:30:02.380
and this is what seeded
the Apache Beam project.

00:30:02.380 --> 00:30:04.510
So in some sense
this is pre-Beam

00:30:04.510 --> 00:30:08.810
and it uses the older com Google
Cloud Dataflow name spaces.

00:30:08.810 --> 00:30:10.180
But it's still fully supported.

00:30:10.180 --> 00:30:12.430
It works great on
Cloud Dataflow.

00:30:12.430 --> 00:30:14.980
You're welcome to use any
of the Apache Beam releases

00:30:14.980 --> 00:30:16.240
on Cloud Dataflow.

00:30:16.240 --> 00:30:19.210
So for Java, that would be the
most recent release which is

00:30:19.210 --> 00:30:20.140
05.

00:30:20.140 --> 00:30:23.490
At least until next
week-ish when 06 comes out.

00:30:23.490 --> 00:30:25.947
And from Python, you'll
have to wait for 06.

00:30:25.947 --> 00:30:27.530
It just merged into
the master branch.

00:30:27.530 --> 00:30:30.220
So it hasn't made a release yet.

00:30:30.220 --> 00:30:32.800
[INAUDIBLE] if you need it.

00:30:32.800 --> 00:30:34.780
And finally, we encourage
you to take a look

00:30:34.780 --> 00:30:37.570
at the next generation
of data SDKs.

00:30:37.570 --> 00:30:39.730
These are based on Apache Beam.

00:30:39.730 --> 00:30:42.670
And what they do is redistribute
the portion of the Apache Beam

00:30:42.670 --> 00:30:46.220
ecosystem that's most
commonly used within GCP.

00:30:46.220 --> 00:30:47.590
All in a single package.

00:30:47.590 --> 00:30:49.925
All in a way that's been
tested to work well together.

00:30:49.925 --> 00:30:51.550
So this is just a
great way for getting

00:30:51.550 --> 00:30:53.830
started with that
broader ecosystem when

00:30:53.830 --> 00:30:56.070
you're running on GCP.

00:30:56.070 --> 00:30:57.660
Now all of these
Beam versions you'll

00:30:57.660 --> 00:31:01.110
notice those version numbers all
come with some sort of caveat.

00:31:01.110 --> 00:31:04.020
The Beam project has not
finalized their API surface

00:31:04.020 --> 00:31:05.920
yet.

00:31:05.920 --> 00:31:07.860
It's been a huge
amount of churn to come

00:31:07.860 --> 00:31:09.690
from the original
dataflow SDK to this.

00:31:09.690 --> 00:31:11.750
We had to rename the packages.

00:31:11.750 --> 00:31:14.310
Every file moved from
Google Cloud Dataflow

00:31:14.310 --> 00:31:15.600
over to Apache Beam.

00:31:15.600 --> 00:31:18.360
So as a user, the kinds of
changes are not that intense,

00:31:18.360 --> 00:31:20.220
but there are still
pretty far reaching.

00:31:20.220 --> 00:31:22.260
So we've been getting all
of those front loaded.

00:31:22.260 --> 00:31:24.580
But the community
has not yet finalized

00:31:24.580 --> 00:31:25.920
that they've completed that.

00:31:25.920 --> 00:31:28.350
So that's actively under
discussion in the community.

00:31:28.350 --> 00:31:30.060
I hope by maybe
early April we'll

00:31:30.060 --> 00:31:32.160
be able to declare
a stable release

00:31:32.160 --> 00:31:34.620
and guarantee that
major versions will not

00:31:34.620 --> 00:31:37.522
be making backwards
incompatible API changes.

00:31:37.522 --> 00:31:38.980
But other than
that, these dataflow

00:31:38.980 --> 00:31:42.619
SDKs are fully ready to run
on Google Cloud Dataflow.

00:31:42.619 --> 00:31:44.910
So if you're interested, you
can go and check them out.

00:31:47.440 --> 00:31:48.810
There you go.

00:31:48.810 --> 00:31:51.850
So this talk is the end
of a wonderful conference.

00:31:51.850 --> 00:31:53.660
There were a number
of other sessions

00:31:53.660 --> 00:31:56.300
during this conference that
were related to Apache Beam.

00:31:56.300 --> 00:31:57.920
If you didn't catch
these in person,

00:31:57.920 --> 00:32:01.550
I really encourage you to
go back and find the videos.

00:32:01.550 --> 00:32:04.070
Tyler's talk from yesterday
goes into more details

00:32:04.070 --> 00:32:05.400
on the Beam model.

00:32:05.400 --> 00:32:09.230
Sort of the evolution and how
it unifies batch and streaming.

00:32:09.230 --> 00:32:11.570
The insights talk
is really about how

00:32:11.570 --> 00:32:13.220
to build a data
processing pipeline

00:32:13.220 --> 00:32:17.120
across all of the technology
in Google Cloud Platform.

00:32:17.120 --> 00:32:21.240
And it includes the use of Beam
and Cloud Dataflow to do that.

00:32:21.240 --> 00:32:23.240
And then finally there
was the talk this morning

00:32:23.240 --> 00:32:26.120
that was really about the
building of the Beam community.

00:32:26.120 --> 00:32:28.460
So we took something
that was a single company

00:32:28.460 --> 00:32:32.210
proprietary project, and we
built a thriving open source

00:32:32.210 --> 00:32:33.417
ecosystem around it.

00:32:33.417 --> 00:32:35.750
And a whole new project under
the umbrella of the Apache

00:32:35.750 --> 00:32:37.040
Software Foundation.

00:32:37.040 --> 00:32:40.150
So really that talk is about
the journey of both the code

00:32:40.150 --> 00:32:45.550
and developers involved, and how
we kick started that community.

00:32:45.550 --> 00:32:48.120
So of course you should
go to Beam the website.

00:32:48.120 --> 00:32:50.330
We've got a lot of
information there.

00:32:50.330 --> 00:32:52.730
You can use it to get started
with any of those runners

00:32:52.730 --> 00:32:54.840
that I showed you today.

00:32:54.840 --> 00:32:56.840
There's also some
great podcast videos,

00:32:56.840 --> 00:33:00.440
other links for learning
more about the Beam model,

00:33:00.440 --> 00:33:02.990
and also of course information
on how to get involved.

00:33:02.990 --> 00:33:05.330
How to come join the
developer mailing list.

00:33:05.330 --> 00:33:08.217
How to start contributing
to the project.

00:33:08.217 --> 00:33:10.550
Tyler wrote some articles for
O'Reilly called "The world

00:33:10.550 --> 00:33:11.300
beyond batch."

00:33:11.300 --> 00:33:14.090
These are also a great way to
learn more about the Beam model

00:33:14.090 --> 00:33:16.557
if you need some airplane
reading for your way home.

00:33:16.557 --> 00:33:18.140
And then finally,
if you want to start

00:33:18.140 --> 00:33:20.870
using Apache Beam on
Cloud Dataflow today,

00:33:20.870 --> 00:33:23.180
you can come and check
out the documentation.

00:33:23.180 --> 00:33:24.440
We'll have pointers there.

00:33:24.440 --> 00:33:26.450
You'll see the site
being in transition.

00:33:26.450 --> 00:33:30.650
So we still fully support the
original pre-Beam releases,

00:33:30.650 --> 00:33:34.960
but we also have a new Beam
ones coming up alongside those.

00:33:34.960 --> 00:33:37.770
All right so with that I'd
love to take some questions.

00:33:47.800 --> 00:33:49.380
AUDIENCE: Hi.

00:33:49.380 --> 00:33:51.990
My team, we do a lot
of stuff in BigQuery.

00:33:51.990 --> 00:33:54.960
But we're hitting a couple of
limitations here and there,

00:33:54.960 --> 00:33:59.370
and looking to merge, sort
of stream and batch directly

00:33:59.370 --> 00:34:02.390
against big storage files.

00:34:02.390 --> 00:34:05.160
But for equivalent
operations, have

00:34:05.160 --> 00:34:07.980
you done any performance
benchmarking versus BigQuery

00:34:07.980 --> 00:34:13.005
and Beam on Dataflow?

00:34:13.005 --> 00:34:15.630
FRANCES PERRY: You know I don't
have the data on that directly,

00:34:15.630 --> 00:34:17.502
but Sam McVeety who's
in the back corner

00:34:17.502 --> 00:34:18.960
there might be able
to help you out

00:34:18.960 --> 00:34:21.929
on those performance questions.

00:34:21.929 --> 00:34:24.070
AUDIENCE: Thank you.

00:34:24.070 --> 00:34:26.030
AUDIENCE: So on
my team we heavily

00:34:26.030 --> 00:34:28.710
build on Spark using Scala
because of the rebel.

00:34:28.710 --> 00:34:30.840
We liked actually
going to the building

00:34:30.840 --> 00:34:32.969
process of seeing how the
data is being transformed

00:34:32.969 --> 00:34:34.739
at every stage.

00:34:34.739 --> 00:34:38.350
And that's something why
we have stopped with Spark.

00:34:38.350 --> 00:34:40.560
Does Beam have any of
those source capabilities

00:34:40.560 --> 00:34:42.179
in the pipeline where
you can actually

00:34:42.179 --> 00:34:43.929
just interact with
your pipeline as you're

00:34:43.929 --> 00:34:46.080
building it similar to Spark?

00:34:46.080 --> 00:34:48.420
FRANCES PERRY: So I think
the Beam model interactive

00:34:48.420 --> 00:34:50.219
is one of those
places as we work

00:34:50.219 --> 00:34:52.440
on sort of pulling
the best patterns out

00:34:52.440 --> 00:34:53.639
of the underlying runners.

00:34:53.639 --> 00:34:56.040
Interactive is something
that's not easily

00:34:56.040 --> 00:34:57.690
done in the Beam
model right now,

00:34:57.690 --> 00:35:00.189
but I think there's definitely
been a lot of interest in it.

00:35:00.189 --> 00:35:02.910
So I'd love to see the community
moving in that direction.

00:35:02.910 --> 00:35:06.180
There is though a Scala SDK
on top of Cloud Dataflow.

00:35:06.180 --> 00:35:09.030
Scala DSL that Spotify
has written called SCIO--

00:35:09.030 --> 00:35:11.690
S-C-I-O. Which is
available on GitHub.

00:35:11.690 --> 00:35:13.267
And you can use on top of.

00:35:13.267 --> 00:35:15.600
I think it may still build
on the current Cloud Dataflow

00:35:15.600 --> 00:35:18.240
release 1.9, but it's moving
towards being built on top

00:35:18.240 --> 00:35:19.489
of Apache Beam going forward.

00:35:19.489 --> 00:35:21.030
So you can definitely
check that out.

00:35:21.030 --> 00:35:23.850
They do have some sort
of rebel base interface

00:35:23.850 --> 00:35:25.739
as well that they
build on top of Beam.

00:35:25.739 --> 00:35:26.280
AUDIENCE: OK.

00:35:26.280 --> 00:35:26.779
Thank you.

00:35:32.290 --> 00:35:34.790
AUDIENCE: I would like to
understand that if the Beam

00:35:34.790 --> 00:35:37.130
community currently
develops a variety

00:35:37.130 --> 00:35:43.890
of the connector or the sync
for storing the [INAUDIBLE]?

00:35:43.890 --> 00:35:45.820
FRANCES PERRY: So
the thing in Beam--

00:35:45.820 --> 00:35:48.742
so we have an I/O-- so there
are plenty of I/O connectors

00:35:48.742 --> 00:35:50.200
out there already
in the ecosystem.

00:35:50.200 --> 00:35:52.110
Hadoop input
basically everything

00:35:52.110 --> 00:35:54.759
can be read as a Hadoop input.

00:35:54.759 --> 00:35:56.800
So we're building a
compatibility layer into Beam

00:35:56.800 --> 00:35:57.312
right now.

00:35:57.312 --> 00:35:59.020
I think it might still
be in for request,

00:35:59.020 --> 00:36:00.100
but it's coming very soon.

00:36:00.100 --> 00:36:01.724
That will allow you
to basically unlock

00:36:01.724 --> 00:36:03.310
any of the Hadoop inputs.

00:36:03.310 --> 00:36:04.330
So those will just run.

00:36:04.330 --> 00:36:07.330
However, Beam actually
has its own I/O API

00:36:07.330 --> 00:36:10.090
that you can use to teach
it about new data sources

00:36:10.090 --> 00:36:10.780
and syncs.

00:36:10.780 --> 00:36:14.920
And it's a little more flexible
than the Hadoop input API.

00:36:14.920 --> 00:36:17.770
So the reason we developed
a new API is that we really

00:36:17.770 --> 00:36:19.750
wanted to give runners
the right hooks

00:36:19.750 --> 00:36:22.460
that they needed to do some
more efficient execution.

00:36:22.460 --> 00:36:24.640
So if you look at
Beam's I/O APIs,

00:36:24.640 --> 00:36:27.100
our source API, what
it allows you to do

00:36:27.100 --> 00:36:30.130
is not just declare how to
divide your input data up

00:36:30.130 --> 00:36:32.140
into x splits and
then read a split.

00:36:32.140 --> 00:36:34.000
It also lets you talk
about the progress

00:36:34.000 --> 00:36:35.950
through reading one
of those splits,

00:36:35.950 --> 00:36:37.420
and how to further subdivide.

00:36:37.420 --> 00:36:40.150
So what that lets the
service do is basically

00:36:40.150 --> 00:36:43.400
decide that because a machine
is misbehaving and slow,

00:36:43.400 --> 00:36:45.880
or because some data is
complex, if it sees a straggler

00:36:45.880 --> 00:36:48.850
shard which is always a huge
problem in these battery

00:36:48.850 --> 00:36:51.239
systems you can go ahead and
subdivide some of that work.

00:36:51.239 --> 00:36:52.780
Steal it from that
worker and give it

00:36:52.780 --> 00:36:54.370
to somebody else who's done.

00:36:54.370 --> 00:36:56.410
But building that
additional flexibility did

00:36:56.410 --> 00:36:58.220
require some
additional API hooks.

00:36:58.220 --> 00:37:00.340
So basically where
Beam's at now is

00:37:00.340 --> 00:37:02.470
you can unlock anything
at sort of the level

00:37:02.470 --> 00:37:04.240
it is elsewhere
in the ecosystem.

00:37:04.240 --> 00:37:06.470
And also we're building
out more and more support

00:37:06.470 --> 00:37:07.720
for these more efficient APIs.

00:37:16.330 --> 00:37:18.377
All right, well wonderful.

